            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.654,
            0.73,
            0.679
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.4 What Allows Life to Be Sustained on Earth?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.694,
            0.744,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Earth has the right conditions for life, but it is the beautiful connections between living and non-living things that help life to thrive. In Curiosity, Grades 6 and 7, you learnt about key natural resources, such as, air, water, sunlight, soil, and minerals. You also learnt about the important life processes in plants and animals. Now, let us explore how all these elements interact to support and sustain life on Earth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.835,
            0.455,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.4.1 Air, water, and sunlight"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.858,
            0.745,
            0.915
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We know that the atmosphere contains oxygen, which humans, animals, and plants use for respiration. In the presence of sunlight, plants take carbon dioxide from the air and water from"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
